5) wc - word count, it tells the no. of lines, words, characters are present in a file
 eg: wc file.txt
result: 15 30 111
15 - no. of lines
30 - total words
111 - no. of characters

6) if we type only "wc", the cursor will wait for us to type the information
- ctrl+D is used to exit the typing
  eg: wc
  type: Hi my name is laasya
  ctrl+d
  result: 1 5 21

7) > - Redirection, it is used to store the output of the command
  eg: wc file.txt > try.txt
      cat try.txt
  - here the the wc output of the file.txt is stored in try.txt, even if the try.txt file does not exist before it will create a file and stores the output
 
  eg: ls file.txt > hi.txt
      cat hi.txt
  - here the list of contents of the file.txt are redirected to hi.txt

8) How to print the middle lines in a file?
  - file.txt has 15 lines, but I wanted to print only line 6,7,8.
  - As we know by using head and tail command we can print the first and last lines in a file and the default number is 10.
  - i.e, we can print only 10 lines at once or we can use head -n, tail -n command to print required lines.
  - But it is not possible to print middle lines.
  - So, this is where piping( | ) command comes. By using piping we can print the middle lines.
    eg: head -8 file.txt | tail -3
    result: line 6
            line 7
            line 8
    - what happens here is head -8 prints first 8 lines, tail -3 takes head -8 as an input and considers printing only the last 3 lines which are line 6,7,8.
    
    eg: head -8 file.txt | tail -3 | wc
    result: 3 6 21

9) ls -l - The ls -l command in Linux is used to list files and directories in the current directory in a long format, displaying detailed information about each file and directory.
eg: drwxr-xr-x 6 laasya555 laasya555 4096 Oct 13 06:10 Adv_c
- drwxr-xr-x - File type and permissions
- 6 - no. of links
- laasya555 - owner
- laasya555 - group
- 4096 - Directory size in bytes
- Oct 13 06:10 -  last modified date & time
- Adv_c - Directory name

10) chmod - change mode, it is used to change the permissions for a file
    eg: chmod 644 file.txt
    result: -rw-r--r-- 1 laasya555 laasya555 111 Oct 13 15:55 file.txt
    
    eg:  chmod 777 file.txt
    result: -rwxrwxrwx 1 laasya555 laasya555  111 Oct 13 15:50 file.txt
    here:
    - r - read
    - w - write
    - x - execute
    - d - directory
    - if the result starts with d then it is the information of directory/folder, else it is a file.

  11) eg: chmod g-x file.txt - used to remove access for group, others
      - what happens here is for g(group) the x(execution) access is removed/removing
 
  12) Shell: A shell is a command-line interpreter that acts as an interface between the user and the operating system.
      - There are 2 types of shells:
        1) Login shell: A login shell is a shell started as part of a user's login session.
           Ctrl+Alt+F3/F4 - open the shell, then it asks for username and password
           Ctrl+Alt+F2 - close/exit
        
